  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDry conditions have returned across the region this \nafternoon as very narrow shortwave ridging, wedged between \nyesterday's upper-level low and an incoming shortwave, builds in. \nThe encroaching shortwave will ensure the brevity of regionwide \ntranquility with lake effect and lake enhanced showers expected to \nbegin this evening in the Adirondacks. Before long, strong west to \nwest-northwesterly flow will allow showers to drift into the Eastern \nCatskills and even as far south and east as the Capital District, \nMid-Hudson Valley, and western New England where moist cyclonic flow \nwill induce orographic enhancement in the higher elevations \nsufficient to sustain showers at an appreciable distance from the \nlake source. Scattered, light showers will linger throughout much of \nthe day tomorrow before dry conditions return in the wake of the \ndeparting shortwave. And, as temperatures are anticipated to remain \nsufficiently above freezing, rain will be the resulting \nprecipitation type. \n\nAnother shortwave ridge builds in and departs, even more swiftly \nthan the last, tomorrow night yielding the approach of yet another \nshortwave trough on Tuesday. Additional showers are therefore \nexpected Tuesday morning through Tuesday night. However, these \nshowers will largely be confined to portions of the Adirondacks, \nwestern Mohawk Valley, northern Catskills, and Southern Greens which \nwill fall within the favored left exit region of the upper-level jet \nembedded within the shortwave. Once again rain looks to be the \npredominant precipitation type, though sub-freezing temperatures in \nthe highest peaks of the Adirondacks early Tuesday morning could \nallow for the mixture of a few snowflakes. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nDeterministic and ensemble guidance continues to \nfavor the development of a large-scale trough across the Eastern \nCONUS towards the end of this week. The introduction of this pattern \nwill bring a cooler trend in comparison to recent weeks. However, \nsince temperatures have largely been above-normal, these \"cooler\" \nconditions will actually fall within our climatological normals for \nthis time of year. \n\nThere also continues to be a resolution by both deterministic and \nensemble models of a storm system developing at the end of this \nweek. There is sufficient agreement in the guidance at this point to \njustify high confidence in the development of a surface low in the \nUpper Midwest by late Tuesday night/early Wednesday morning. This \nlow will then track into the Ohio Valley during the day Wednesday. \nHowever, this is the extent of the unambiguous details of the \nevolution of this system as the guidance is highly varied in its \ndepiction of its track and associated timing. The weak trend at this \npoint depicts a southerly track of either the same cyclone, or a \nsecondary one that overtakes this primary, into the Mid-Atlantic \nsuch that a Miller-B type Nor'easter forms. However, that same \nweakly favored track takes the low far enough to the south that we \nsustain little precipitation in comparison to the northerly outlier. \nWe will continue to monitor trends closely over the upcoming week \nand provide updates as details become more clear.",
